@chapter 9. Codex Security

Source: https://github.com/openai/codex-security · Site: GitHub · Submitter: bakigul (Hacker
News) · Submitted: 2026-07-28 20:52 UTC (Hacker News) · HN activity: 529 points · 191 comments ·
Length: 187 words (~1 min read) · Language: en

@@openai/codex-security is a CLI and TypeScript SDK for finding, validating, and fixing security
vulnerabilities in your code. Scan repositories, review changes, track findings over time, and
run security checks in CI.

Documentation

Quick start

Requires Node.js 22 or later, Python 3.10 or later, and access to Codex Security.

npm install @openai/codex-security

npx codex-security login

npx codex-security scan .

For CI, set OPENAI_API_KEY instead of signing in.

If both a ChatGPT sign-in and an API key are available, interactive scans ask which credential
to use. CI and other noninteractive scans keep the existing API-key precedence. Select a
credential explicitly when needed:

npx codex-security scan . --auth chatgpt

npx codex-security scan . --auth api-key

To make your ChatGPT sign-in the automatic default, unset any configured API keys:

unset OPENAI_API_KEY CODEX_API_KEY

Scan history is stored in the Codex Security workbench state directory. If that directory cannot
be written, set CODEX_SECURITY_STATE_DIR to a writable directory outside the repository.

TypeScript SDK

import { CodexSecurity } from "@openai/codex-security";

const security = new CodexSecurity();

const result = await security.run(".");

console.log(result.reportPath);

await security.close();

For installation, authentication, scan options, and CI setup, see the official documentation.

@chapter 10. They Stole Your Attention

Source: https://moai.studio/blog/posts/they-stole-your-attention.html · Site: moai.studio ·
Submitter: ionwake (Hacker News) · Submitted: 2026-07-29 11:28 UTC (Hacker News) · HN activity:
23 points · 28 comments · Length: 638 words (~3 min read) · Language: en

Trillion-dollar machines compete for your next 47 seconds. Here are the logs.

> the feed is a slot machine that fits in a pocket

> each scroll is a pull, each notification a bell

> the reward is randomised on purpose. randomness is what hooks.

> an AI tunes the feed against the weakest moments of the day

> it learns the triggers faster than the user does

> attention is the scarce resource of the age

> and it gets strip-mined for ad revenue

> deep work feels impossible because it's being out-competed

> willpower loses to a machine built to beat it

> the lever that works is removing the machine

> reclaim the input, the output follows

Seconds of attention on one screen before switching

Logged screen focus, knowledge workers & students · Gloria Mark, UC Irvine

data table

year avg seconds

2004 ~150

2012 75

2016 47 (median 40)

2025 47, reaffirmed (replications 44–50)

Measured by unobtrusive computer logging, workplace and home. The famous “goldfish attention
span” stat is folklore; these are logged durations. The 2025 column marks Mark's January 2025
reaffirmation: the average has held at 47 seconds since ~2016, a steady state. Whisker shows the
40–50s band across the median and independent replications.

> UC Irvine has logged screen focus for twenty years

> 2004: two and a half minutes on one screen

> 2012: 75 seconds

> 2016: 47 seconds. median 40.

> 2025: still 47.

> the machine only has to win once every 47 seconds

The interruption tax

Focus interval vs cost to re-immerse · Gloria Mark, UC Irvine

data table

quantity time

avg focus on one screen 47 s

re-immersion after a switch 25 min 26 s

Logged averages: 47 seconds of focus per screen; 25 minutes 26 seconds to fully re-immerse in
the original task after a diversion.

> microsoft telemetry, 2025: the most-pinged fifth of workers eat an interruption every 2
minutes

> 117 emails a day. 85% skimmed in under 15 seconds.

An 8-hour day, one tick per interruption

Microsoft 365 telemetry, 2025 · most-pinged 20% of workers

data table

metric value

interruption cadence, core hours every 2 min

interruptions per 24h 275

emails received/day (typical) 117

emails read in under 15s 85%

Rolling 28-day Microsoft 365 telemetry ending Feb 2025, top 20% of users by ping volume: meeting
invites, emails and chats land every 2 minutes across the 9–5.

What one year of your attention sells for

Meta ad revenue per user, US & Canada, $/year · company filings

data table

year $/user

Annual average revenue per user, US & Canada segment, compiled from Meta quarterly filings. Meta
moved the denominator from Facebook-only users to Family of Apps users in 2021; historical
figures were restated. Full-year 2025 worldwide figure: $57.03 per user, an all-time high.

> meta earned $17 per north american user in 2011

> $233 in 2024. 13x in 13 years.

> 2025 worldwide: $57 a head, an all-time high

> teens: median 237 notifications a day. a quarter land during school hours.

> humanity spent 5.3 trillion hours inside apps in 2025

5.3Thours in apps, 2025 (Sensor Tower)

3.6havg time in apps per user per day, 2025

237median daily notifications, US teens (Common Sense, 2023)

~25minto re-immerse in a task after a diversion (Mark, UC Irvine)

275daily interruptions for the most-pinged 20% of workers (Microsoft 365 telemetry, 2025)

Chart & stat sources: Gloria Mark, APA interview · Univ. of California · Common Sense Media,
Constant Companion (2023) · Sensor Tower, State of Mobile 2026 · Microsoft WorkLab, Infinite
Workday (2025) · Meta investor relations

The Verdict

Phone in another room. Feeds off the work machine. Keep blocks of hours no ping can reach.

